cvi_vc_drv: join the VDEC event-handler kthread before freeing the channel

CVI_VDEC_DestroyChn() vfree()s the vdec_chn_context, and struct semaphore
sem_getframe is *embedded* in that context.  vdec_event_handler(), the
cvitask_vdec_%d kthread, spends its life asleep in down_timeout() on exactly
that semaphore.  The join that should have made this safe was conditional:

    if (IF_WANNA_DISABLE_BIND_MODE()) {      /* currBindMode && !enable_bind_mode */
            ... kthread_stop(pVbCtx->thread) ...
    }

Both flags belong to sys_ctx_bind()/sys_ctx_unbind() in the sys module and
describe the VDEC->VO *binding*, not the kthread.  enable_bind_mode is only
cleared by an explicit CVI_SYS_UnBind(), so a producer that is stopped or
killed without unbinding first destroys its channel with the flag still set,
the test is false, the join is skipped -- and the MEM_FREE() a few lines below
pulls the semaphore out from under a thread that is still sleeping on it.

On badge #1 that skip is not a race, it is the only possible outcome, and this
follows from the source alone: vdec_vb_ctx[] is a module global in cv181x_sys
that holds whatever enable_bind_mode an earlier bind left in it, and our VO
producer (scripts/badge_landscape_vo.c) runs with BLVO_BIND unset, so it calls
neither CVI_SYS_Bind() nor CVI_SYS_UnBind().  The handler thread in the trace
below can only have been started by IF_WANNA_ENABLE_BIND_MODE(), i.e. with the
flag already set -- and with no process unbinding, nothing can clear it before
the destroy.  Hence a skipped join on every teardown, which is exactly the
reported "oopses on every systemctl stop/restart of the producer".

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address ffff800084b7d620
    FSC = 0x07: level 3 translation fault      (pte=0 -- the page is gone)
    pc : _raw_spin_lock_irq+0x40/0x80
    lr : __down_common+0x150/0x430
  Call trace:
    _raw_spin_lock_irq+0x40/0x80 (P)
    __down_timeout+0x20/0x40
    down_timeout+0x74/0xa8
    vdec_event_handler+0x12c/0x3c0 [cvi_vc_driver]
    kthread+0x134/0x150
  CPU: 0 PID: 1858 Comm: cvitask_vdec_0
  note: cvitask_vdec_0[1858] exited with irqs disabled
  note: cvitask_vdec_0[1858] exited with preempt_count 1

It faults inside the semaphore's own spinlock with interrupts already off, so
the thread dies holding them: the box is degraded until it is rebooted.  On
the badge this reproduces on every systemctl stop/restart of the VO producer.

Tie the handler's lifetime to the channel instead of to the bind state:

 * _cvi_vdec_stop_event_handler() -- claim pVbCtx->thread, up() the semaphore
   to release a handler that is blocked right now, kthread_stop() to ask it to
   stop and wait for it to return, then clear currBindMode.  Called
   unconditionally from CVI_VDEC_DestroyChn() before any free.  Safe when no
   thread was started and safe on a double destroy (the thread pointer is
   taken first, and check_vdec_chn_handle() already rejects the second call).

 * vdec_event_handler() re-checks kthread_should_stop() after every wait, and
   before the wait inside the retry loop.  down_timeout() sleeps in
   TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE, so kthread_stop()'s wakeup does not abort it; the
   join is bounded by the existing one-second semaphore timeout.
   The dead "pChnHandle == NULL -> channel destroyed" test it used to rely on
   is replaced by a comment saying why it could never have fired: pChnHandle
   is the thread's own local copy of the pointer, not the slot in
   vdec_handle->chn_handle[].

 * The handler no longer returns on its own.  kthread_create() takes no
   reference on the task_struct it returns, so a kthread that leaves threadfn
   is reaped and freed and the later kthread_stop() would itself be a
   use-after-free; the two start-up failure paths now park until they are
   stopped.

 * CVI_VDEC_SendStream() only starts a handler when there is not one already
   (pVbCtx->thread == NULL), and checks kthread_run() for IS_ERR before
   storing the pointer or passing it to the scheduler.  Previously a failed
   kthread_run() stored an ERR_PTR that the teardown path would then treat as
   a thread, and a second bind-mode transition could strand a running handler.

 * sema_init() moves to CVI_VDEC_CreateChn() so sem_getframe is valid for the
   whole life of the channel rather than only from the first bind-mode
   SendStream().  up() on the all-zero image MEM_CALLOC() (vzalloc) leaves
   behind reads wait_list.next == NULL, concludes the list is not empty and
   dereferences it.

